::: {.doc-subject}

Attach a narration to the next recorded step.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L1108-L1199){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.note(text)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Every recorded step (see `steps`) carries a short narration that is displayed alongside
the code when the step log is replayed with `play()`. By default, narrations are generated
automatically (e.g., "Edge A → B added."). `note()` lets you replace that default with your
own explanation, which is handy when building teaching material, walkthroughs, or
reproducible demos where the *why* of each step matters more than the *what*.

The note is held as pending and consumed by the next operation that records a step; that
step's narration becomes `text=` instead of the auto-generated one. Operations that don't
record steps (such as `count_nodes()`) leave the note pending. If `note()` is called again
while a note is still pending, the earlier note is flushed as a standalone `"annotate"`
step before the new one is stored. Calling `play()` also flushes a pending note as a
standalone step, so a trailing note still appears at the end of the replay. The graph
structure itself is never changed.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[text]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The narration text to display during replay. It replaces the auto-generated narration
    of the next recorded step (or becomes the narration of a standalone annotation step,
    as described above).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Returns self to enable method chaining.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~steps`) 

:   The step log that holds the narrations.

[](`~play`) 

:   Replay the log, showing each step's narration.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Notes slot into a build chain just before the step they describe. Steps without a
preceding note keep their auto-generated narration:

::: {#b0b981ad .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = (
    ec.Graph()
    .note("Start with the two founders.")
    .add_nodes(["Ana", "Ben"])
    .add_edge("Ana", "Ben")
    .note("Ben recruits Cy.")
    .add_node("Cy")
    .add_edge("Ben", "Cy")
)

graph.steps.to_polars().select("method", "narration")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (4, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>method</th><th>narration</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;add_nodes&quot;</td><td>&quot;Start with the two founders.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;add_edge&quot;</td><td>&quot;Edge Ana → Ben added.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;add_node&quot;</td><td>&quot;Ben recruits Cy.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;add_edge&quot;</td><td>&quot;Edge Ben → Cy added.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


Calling `note()` twice in a row turns the first note into its own `"annotate"` step,
since no operation consumed it. The second note stays pending for the next step:

::: {#ba23425f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.note("The team is complete.").note("Now let's analyze it.")

graph.steps[-1].kind, graph.steps[-1].narration
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
('annotate', 'The team is complete.')
```
:::
:::


### Narrating a replay

Notes are what `play()` shows under each frame, so they turn a replay into an explanation.
Here each step of a short metro analysis says why it was run:

::: {#939c3250 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
metro = ec.metro()

metro.note("Which links would split the network if they closed?")
metro.bridges()
metro.note("How does the airport connect to the city center?")
metro.shortest_path("Central", "Airport")

metro.play(layout="pos")
```

